/-!
# A stalkwise criterion for Koszul-regularity of a closed immersion

Let `i : Z ⟶ S` be a closed immersion into a locally Noetherian scheme and fix a natural number
`r`.  Suppose that at every point `z` of `Z` the kernel of `O_{S, i z} ⟶ O_{Z, z}` is generated
by a weakly regular sequence of length exactly `r`.  Then `i` is a Koszul-regular immersion, and
therefore a local complete intersection morphism.

The length has to be the *same* `r` at every point.  Without that the statement is false for the
Koszul condition: two generators of a principal ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor have a Koszul
complex with nonvanishing `H₁`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf_of_stalk_isWeaklyRegular`:
  the criterion for the defining ideal sheaf of a closed immersion on the target itself.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isKoszulRegularImmersion_of_stalk_isWeaklyRegular`:
  the criterion for Koszul-regularity of the immersion.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isLocalCompleteIntersectionMorphism_of_stalk_isWeaklyRegular`: the resulting
  local complete intersection morphism.

## Implementation notes

Koszul-regularity of an ideal sheaf is a *germwise* condition
(`isKoszulSequenceExactOn_iff_forall_germ_isKoszulRegular`), so the whole argument reduces to
commutative algebra in the local rings, and no sheaf-level Koszul complex is needed.

At a point `x` of the support, the hypothesis supplies a weakly regular generating sequence `g`
of length `r` of the stalk ideal.  It is realised by `r` sections of the defining ideal sheaf on
a neighbourhood (`exists_sections_mem_stalkIdeal_germFamily_eq`), and Nakayama spreading
(`exists_isGeneratedByOn_definingIdealOfHom`, which is where local finite presentation and hence
the Noetherian hypothesis enter) shrinks that neighbourhood to one `U'` on which those sections
generate.  At a point `y` of `U'` the germs of those sections therefore generate the stalk ideal
at `y`, and they are `r` of them:

* if `y` lies in the support, the hypothesis at `y` supplies another weakly regular — hence
  Koszul-regular — generating sequence of the same length `r`, and Stacks tag 066A
  (`Koszul.regularity_of_generators_of_eq_span_range`) transfers Koszul-regularity between two
  generating sequences of equal length of one ideal;
* if `y` does not lie in the support, the stalk ideal is the unit ideal, so one of the germs is
  a unit of the local ring and the sequence is Koszul-regular for that reason
  (`koszulRegular_of_span_eq_top`).

This is why no minimality or conormal-rank argument is needed: tag 066A already compares two
generating sequences of the same length, which is exactly what fixing `r` provides.

The conditions of Stacks tag 063J are stated on the canonical open `i.coborderRange`, so the
criterion is proved for an arbitrary closed immersion and then applied to the canonical closed
factor `i.liftCoborder`.  The hypothesis is carried across the identification of local rings
`Stacks.Conormal.coborderStalkEquiv`, under which the two stalk maps agree.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Definition 31.21.2 (Tag 063D)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063D)
* [Stacks Project, Definition 31.21.4 (Tag 063J)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063J)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 15.30.3 (Tag 066A)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/066A)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 15.30.1 (Tag 062F)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/062F)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 37.62.11 (Tag 069L)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/069L)
-/

